/**
\class pdg::Scene
\ingroup Animation
\brief Own layers, an isolated physics world, a camera, timers and simulation time.

Create gameplay first and HUD second: scenes draw in construction order above
legacy layers. Both can target the same Port. Pausing gameplay keeps drawing its
held pose while the HUD continues to animate and receive its own timer events.
Raw input is controlled separately with setInputEnabled(boolean).

addLayer(layer) adopts a detached layer or a layer from the legacy factory.
removeLayer(layer) detaches without destroying sprites or their physical state;
the caller must attach it elsewhere or clean it up. disposeLayer(layer) destroys
an attached layer. dispose() is idempotent, cancels timers and subscriptions, and
destroys remaining attached layers. Detached layers survive. Cross-layer physics
constraints must be disconnected before detachment. Mutation of layer membership
must occur outside scene traversal. Deferred disposal is safe inside callbacks.

The effective layer camera is its explicit override, otherwise its Scene camera,
otherwise its Port camera. Scene cameras and layer overrides advance once on the
owning scene clock. A camera cannot be automatically advanced by two scenes.

advance(seconds), setFixedStep(seconds), getSimulationTime() and getDroppedTime()
use seconds. setManual(true) disables automatic host advancement. Fixed step 0
selects variable stepping; a positive value selects fixed stepping. Updates admit
at most 0.25 seconds of host time and run at most eight fixed steps. Excess whole
steps are discarded and reported by getDroppedTime(); discarded time does not
fire logical timers. setTimeScale(scale) accepts finite nonnegative values. Zero
freezes advancement without setting isPaused(). Resume adds no paused-time debt.

setInterpolation(true) enables read-only interpolation of sprite root translation,
scale and shortest-path rotation during fixed-step rendering. Mounted sprites
inherit the parent correction. Queries and physics retain authoritative state;
skeletal poses and camera poses use current state. A teleport resets presentation.

Scene timer delays and intervals use milliseconds. IDs are positive and local to
each scene. onTimeout(callback, delayMs) and onInterval(callback, intervalMs) return
cancellable handles. Callbacks receive the timer event synchronously. Repeating
intervals must be positive; zero-delay one-shots wait for the next update. Timers
fire before animation at an update boundary, at most once per step, and newly
created timers wait for a later boundary. Scene pause includes timers created
while paused. Scene resume preserves individually paused timers. Scaling affects
logical deadlines; getWhenTimerFiresNext(id) returns the logical deadline in
milliseconds, or -1 for an absent or paused timer.

subscribe(emitter, eventType, callback) returns a cancellable external registration
and disconnects it on explicit dispose(). Pause does not suppress raw external
events. Async work and sound servicing continue independently; applications decide
when to apply late results and pause gameplay voices.

\note Rendering requires a graphics-capable build. Manual simulation, timers and
camera animation are available headlessly. Explicitly dispose scenes when leaving
a level; garbage collection is not a lifecycle boundary.

\code{.js}
const gameplay = new pdg.Scene();
gameplay.setFixedStep(1 / 60);
gameplay.setInterpolation(true);
const hud = new pdg.Scene();
const worldLayer = gameplay.createSpriteLayer();
const hudLayer = hud.createSpriteLayer();
worldLayer.setSpritePort(port);
hudLayer.setSpritePort(port);
hud.onInterval(function () { caret.setOpacity(caret.getOpacity() ? 0 : 1); }, 500);
gameplay.pause();     // HUD continues above the held gameplay image.
gameplay.resume();
// On leaving this level:
gameplay.dispose();
hud.dispose();
\endcode
*/
